    "cautions": "The statements below come from the prescribing information of Ozempic and of Wegovy, two semaglutide products that the listing does not say this item is, and from two FDA pages, one current as of October 1, 2026 and one as of July 26, 2024. None of these documents names this item, and none says how far its statements apply to it.\n\nBoth labels carry a boxed warning on the risk of thyroid C-cell tumors. Each states that in rodents semaglutide causes dose-dependent and treatment-duration-dependent thyroid C-cell tumors at clinically relevant exposures, and that it is unknown whether the product causes such tumors, including medullary thyroid carcinoma, in humans, as the human relevance of the rodent tumors has not been determined. Each states that the product is contraindicated in patients with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or with Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia syndrome type 2.\n\nEach label also lists as a contraindication a serious hypersensitivity reaction to semaglutide or to any of the product's excipients, and says serious hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis and angioedema, have been reported. Both labels carry warnings whose headings name acute pancreatitis, acute gallbladder disease, acute kidney injury due to volume depletion, severe gastrointestinal adverse reactions, hypersensitivity reactions, diabetic retinopathy complications, hypoglycemia, and pulmonary aspiration during general anesthesia or deep sedation. The Wegovy labeling also carries a warning on heart rate increase. The labels' warnings about sharing pens are not carried, since the listing is a vial.\n\nFDA states on its alert page that compounded drugs are not FDA approved, which it says means the agency does not review them for safety, effectiveness or quality before they are marketed, and that such drugs should be used only in patients whose medical needs no FDA-approved drug can meet. The listing does not say how this item is prepared.\n\nFDA states that it has received multiple reports of adverse events, some requiring hospitalization, that may be related to dosing errors associated with compounded injectable semaglutide products, and that the errors resulted from patients measuring and self-administering incorrect doses and, in some cases, from health care professionals miscalculating doses. FDA's 2024 page adds that product concentrations may vary, and that unfamiliarity with withdrawing medication from a vial into a syringe, coupled with confusion between different units of measurement, may have contributed to such errors.\n\nFDA states that it has received adverse event reports that may be related to patients being prescribed compounded semaglutide or tirzepatide products in doses beyond what is in the FDA-approved drug label. It says some of the adverse events are serious, and that some patients reported seeking medical attention for symptoms including nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain and constipation. FDA says it encourages patients to talk with their health care provider or compounder about how to measure and administer the intended dose of compounded semaglutide or tirzepatide.\n\nFDA states that it is aware that compounded GLP-1 medications may be dispensed in vials containing multiple doses for a patient to self-administer, and that it generally recommends that vials containing multiple doses of sterile medications be used within 28 days after the first use, because the risk of contamination increases after 28 days. Where medication remains in such a vial 28 days after the first use, FDA recommends that consumers discard the vial even if medication remains, not continue using it even if instructions from the compounder say it can be used for longer than 28 days, and always use a new, sterile needle and syringe for each dose. The listing's row does not say how many doses the vial holds.\n\nFDA states that injectable GLP-1 drugs require refrigeration as indicated in their package inserts, that it has received complaints that certain compounded GLP-1 drugs have arrived warm or with inadequate ice packs, and that it recommends patients not use any injectable GLP-1 drug that arrives warm or with insufficient refrigeration, as this can affect the drug's quality. The listing's row does not say at what temperature the item is to be kept.\n\nFDA states that, as of May 31, 2026, it had received 990 reports of adverse events associated with compounded semaglutide. It says such events are likely underreported, and that it is not always possible to determine whether an event resulted directly from the drug or whether other factors contributed.\n\nFDA states on its page current as of October 1, 2026 that some semaglutide products sold by compounders may be salt forms, including semaglutide sodium and semaglutide acetate, that these are not the active ingredient used in the approved drugs, that it has no information on whether they have the same chemical and pharmacologic properties as that ingredient, and that it is not aware of any lawful basis for their use in compounding. The listing's row does not say which form of semaglutide the item holds.\n\nFDA stated on its 2024 page that it is aware that some compounders incorporate additional ingredients, such as cyanocobalamin (vitamin B-12), into their semaglutide products, and that the safety and effectiveness of combining semaglutide with other ingredients has not been established.\n\nNone of these statements names this item. The listing's row gives names and figures and calls the item a vial; it does not state the route, the form of each ingredient or what else the vial holds, and the dispensed product's own label and the prescribing clinician govern its use.",
    "contentReviewReceiptSha256": "fd852ee08fc4c351f0325021d8f4435e5b741a2641ecac68c8ff9c51800d1c92",
