    "datasetSha256": "76b9bf6ffe908450d31e15900ee9aa1a5467068fd3dfecb2d28624a49c97c4d5",
    "family": "clinical_formulations_503a",
    "offeringId": "mo_d91faa561e8eeec03b3e",
    "sourceVariantId": "mov_b3ab2236ef6c69a31d69",
    "holdCode": "risk_review",
    "holdMessage": "Details for this listing have not yet been confirmed; a quote request is a question to our team and is not an order, a reservation or a prescription.",
    "summary": "A vial listed as semaglutide with B12, 2.2 mg-500 mcg/mL (2 mL) (4.4 mg vial). Semaglutide activates the receptor for GLP-1, one of the body's own hormones. No label or FDA application product found combines semaglutide with B12; the labels of the two semaglutide medicines cited carry a boxed warning on thyroid C-cell tumors. Clinical review required.",
    "overview": "In plain terms, the catalog lists this item as semaglutide with B12, which this page reads as vitamin B12, and calls it a vial; it gives the figures 2.2 mg-500 mcg/mL (2 mL) (4.4 mg vial) and does not say in words which figure belongs to which ingredient. Semaglutide is the active ingredient of two medicines whose labels this page cites, Ozempic and Wegovy; they say it binds to and activates the GLP-1 receptor, the receptor the body's own GLP-1 acts on. The Ozempic label describes GLP-1 as a hormone with multiple actions on glucose and the Wegovy label as a regulator of appetite and caloric intake; both are labels of other finished products, and their labeled uses are not labeled uses of this item. A National Institutes of Health fact sheet says vitamin B12 is a nutrient that helps keep the body's blood and nerve cells healthy and helps make DNA. No label found and no product in FDA's application data combines semaglutide with vitamin B12, no document cited here says what the combination does, and FDA said in 2024 that the safety and effectiveness of combining semaglutide with other ingredients has not been established. Both labels carry a boxed warning on the risk of thyroid C-cell tumors, and FDA says it has received multiple reports of adverse events, some requiring hospitalization, that may be related to dosing errors with versions of semaglutide that have not had its review; the cautions give these and FDA's other statements in full. A licensed clinician reviews each request and decides whether it is appropriate.\n\nThe catalog lists this item as semaglutide with B12 and gives the figures 2.2 mg-500 mcg/mL (2 mL) (4.4 mg vial). The listing's row gives those names and figures and calls the item a vial; it does not say which form of semaglutide or of B12 the item holds, the route, or what else the vial holds. After the two names the row gives two figures joined by a hyphen; it does not say in words which figure belongs to which name, and this page prints them as listed. This page reads B12 as vitamin B12; the row does not spell it out.\n\nA search of DailyMed drug labels for this page, run on November 11, 2026 (UTC), looked up the name semaglutide and returned nine labels. Each codes semaglutide as its only active ingredient, and none lists vitamin B12. FDA's drug application data, read the same day, list 34 semaglutide products, each with semaglutide as its only active ingredient. So this page has no labeling of a product like the listed one to cite, and cites no labeled indication for the item.\n\nThis page cites the prescribing information of Ozempic (semaglutide injection) and of Wegovy (semaglutide injection and tablets), in the versions on DailyMed that day (effective June 1, 2026 and June 18, 2026). Each is the labeling of another finished product, which the listing does not say this item is, and neither names vitamin B12. Their labeled uses differ from each other and are not carried here.\n\nBoth say that semaglutide acts as a GLP-1 receptor agonist that selectively binds to and activates the GLP-1 receptor, the target for native GLP-1. The Ozempic labeling describes GLP-1 as a physiological hormone that has multiple actions on glucose; the Wegovy labeling describes it as a physiological regulator of appetite and caloric intake. Both say semaglutide stimulates insulin secretion in a glucose-dependent manner.\n\nA National Institutes of Health fact sheet for consumers says vitamin B12 is a nutrient that helps keep the body's blood and nerve cells healthy and helps make DNA. It does not mention semaglutide. No document cited here says what semaglutide combined with vitamin B12 does. FDA said on a page current as of July 26, 2024 that some compounders incorporate additional ingredients into their semaglutide products and that the safety and effectiveness of combining semaglutide with other ingredients has not been established; the cautions give its words.\n\nFDA keeps an alert page on versions of semaglutide and tirzepatide that have not had FDA's review, current as of October 1, 2026. The cautions carry what it says about doses and how they are measured, vials that hold multiple doses, keeping injectable GLP-1 drugs cold, and reports of adverse events. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.\n\nThis listing does not state who prepares or supplies the item, how it is prepared, or whether it holds other ingredients; the dispensed product's own label governs. A prescription is required. Requests go through the provider pathway, where a licensed clinician decides whether it is appropriate. Fulfillment depends on state availability and pharmacy requirements, and it is not available for direct purchase.",
    "purpose": "The DailyMed search for this page returned no prescribing information for a product that combines semaglutide with vitamin B12, so this page cites no labeled indication for the item. The two labels it cites are for semaglutide products that hold no vitamin B12; their labeled uses are uses of those products and are not labeled uses of this item. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.",
