    "summary": "A vial listed as tirzepatide with B12, 10.5 mg-500 mcg/mL (5 mL) (52.5 mg vial). Tirzepatide activates two receptors, the ones the body's own GIP and GLP-1 act on. No label or FDA application product found combines tirzepatide with B12; the labels of the two tirzepatide medicines cited carry a boxed warning on thyroid C-cell tumors. Clinical review required.",
    "overview": "In plain terms, the catalog lists this item as tirzepatide with B12, which this page reads as vitamin B12, and calls it a vial; it gives the figures 10.5 mg-500 mcg/mL (5 mL) (52.5 mg vial) and does not say in words which figure belongs to which ingredient. Tirzepatide is the active ingredient of two medicines whose labels this page cites, Mounjaro and Zepbound; they say it binds to and activates the receptors that the body's own GIP and GLP-1 act on. The Mounjaro label says tirzepatide enhances insulin secretion and reduces glucagon levels, both in a glucose-dependent manner, and the Zepbound label describes GLP-1 as a regulator of appetite and caloric intake; both are labels of other finished products, and their labeled uses are not labeled uses of this item. A National Institutes of Health fact sheet says vitamin B12 is a nutrient that helps keep the body's blood and nerve cells healthy and helps make DNA. No label found and no product in FDA's application data combines tirzepatide with vitamin B12, and no document cited here says what the combination does. Both labels carry a boxed warning on the risk of thyroid C-cell tumors, and FDA says it has received adverse event reports, some of them serious, that may be related to patients being prescribed versions that have not had its review in doses beyond what is in the FDA-approved drug label; the cautions give these and FDA's other statements in full. A licensed clinician reviews each request and decides whether it is appropriate.\n\nThe catalog lists this item as tirzepatide with B12 and gives the figures 10.5 mg-500 mcg/mL (5 mL) (52.5 mg vial). The listing's row gives those names and figures and calls the item a vial; it does not say which form of tirzepatide or of B12 the item holds, the route, or what else the vial holds. After the two names the row gives two figures joined by a hyphen; it does not say in words which figure belongs to which name, and this page prints them as listed. This page reads B12 as vitamin B12; the row does not spell it out.\n\nA search of DailyMed drug labels for this page, run on November 11, 2026 (UTC), looked up the name tirzepatide and returned eight labels. Each codes tirzepatide as its only active ingredient, and none lists vitamin B12. FDA's drug application data, read the same day, list 48 tirzepatide products, each with tirzepatide as its only active ingredient. So this page has no labeling of a product like the listed one to cite, and cites no labeled indication for the item.\n\nThis page cites the prescribing information of Mounjaro and of Zepbound (both tirzepatide injections), in the versions on DailyMed that day (effective August 27, 2026 and August 28, 2026). Each is the labeling of another finished product, which the listing does not say this item is, and neither names vitamin B12. Their labeled uses differ from each other and are not carried here.\n\nBoth say that tirzepatide is a GIP receptor and GLP-1 receptor agonist that selectively binds to and activates both the GIP and GLP-1 receptors, the targets for native GIP and GLP-1. The Zepbound labeling describes GLP-1 as a physiological regulator of appetite and caloric intake. The Mounjaro labeling says tirzepatide enhances insulin secretion and reduces glucagon levels, both in a glucose-dependent manner; the Zepbound labeling says it stimulates insulin secretion in a glucose-dependent manner and reduces glucagon secretion.\n\nA National Institutes of Health fact sheet for consumers says vitamin B12 is a nutrient that helps keep the body's blood and nerve cells healthy and helps make DNA. It does not mention tirzepatide. No document cited here describes tirzepatide combined with vitamin B12 as a product or says what the combination does, so this page says nothing about it.\n\nFDA keeps an alert page on versions of semaglutide and tirzepatide that have not had FDA's review, current as of October 1, 2026. The cautions carry what it says about doses and how they are measured, vials that hold multiple doses, keeping injectable GLP-1 drugs cold, and reports of adverse events. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.\n\nThis listing does not state who prepares or supplies the item, how it is prepared, or whether it holds other ingredients; the dispensed product's own label governs. A prescription is required. Requests go through the provider pathway, where a licensed clinician decides whether it is appropriate. Fulfillment depends on state availability and pharmacy requirements, and it is not available for direct purchase.",
    "purpose": "The DailyMed search for this page returned no prescribing information for a product that combines tirzepatide with vitamin B12, so this page cites no labeled indication for the item. The two labels it cites are for tirzepatide products that hold no vitamin B12; their labeled uses are uses of those products and are not labeled uses of this item. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.",
