    "cautions": "The statements below are FDA's, from its page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks, which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page. They are about the substances FDA names and drugs that contain them; FDA's entries name no particular product, and none speaks of these substances combined.\n\nOn BPC-157: FDA states that compounded drugs containing BPC-157 may pose risk for immunogenicity for certain routes of administration and may have complexities with regard to peptide-related impurities and the characterization of the active pharmaceutical ingredient. It states that it has identified no, or only limited, safety-related information for the proposed routes of administration, and that it therefore lacks sufficient information to know whether the drug would cause harm when administered to humans.\n\nOn the thymosin beta-4 fragment also known as TB-500: FDA states that compounded drugs containing the thymosin beta-4 fragment may pose risk for immunogenicity for certain routes of administration due to the potential for aggregation as well as peptide-related impurities. It states that it has not identified any human exposure data on drug products containing the fragment, and that it lacks important information regarding any safety issues raised by this drug, including whether it would cause harm if administered to humans.\n\nFDA places each of these entries in the list its page heads 'Bulk drug substances nominated but withdrawn', and describes that list as bulk drug substances previously in category 2 of the interim policies that were withdrawn by the nominators. This page reads that as the nomination having been withdrawn by those who made it; FDA's page still gives a statement for each entry under its heading 'Potential significant safety risks'. The same page says that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nThe listing's row names BPC-157 and TB500; it does not state the item's sterility or what else it holds, and the dispensed product's own label and the prescribing clinician govern its use.",
