    "holdMessage": "Details for this listing have not yet been confirmed; a quote request is a question to our team and is not an order, a reservation or a prescription.",
    "summary": "GHK-Cu/Epithalon 10-2 mg/mL (5 mL), as listed. FDA lists each among bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks. A label search found no prescribing information, and a prescription is required. Clinical review required.",
    "overview": "In plain terms, the catalog lists this item as GHK-Cu/Epithalon 10-2 mg/mL (5 mL); the row does not say in words which figure belongs to which name, or what else the item holds. A label search found no prescribing information, so this page states no use for the item and does not say how it works. FDA lists each among bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks; what each entry says is set out below, and in full in the cautions. A licensed clinician reviews each request and decides whether it is appropriate.\n\nThe catalog lists this item as GHK-Cu/Epithalon and gives the figures 10-2 mg/mL (5 mL). After the names the row gives figures joined by a hyphen; it does not say in words which figure belongs to which name, and this page prints them as listed. The row does not state the route or what else the item holds; the catalog counts the item in vials in its per-fill limit.\n\nA search of DailyMed drug labels for this page, run on November 11, 2026 (UTC), looked up a name for each listed substance and returned five entries, all under GHK-Cu and one other name for it searched, each a non-prescription product label for another product. None is prescribing information, and this page cites none of them. FDA's drug application data, read the same day, returned no product with an active ingredient of any name searched. This page has no labeled indication to cite for the item; it cites no document about the substances but FDA's page and makes no statement of its own about what the item, or any substance named in it, is used for or how it works.\n\nFDA's page 'Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks', which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page, has an entry under each of the two listed names or a form of it, headed 'GHK-Cu (for injectable routes of administration)' and 'Epitalon'. The listing's row spells the name Epithalon; this page reads the two spellings as one name, and that reading is its own.\n\nEach of these entries is in the list the page heads 'Bulk drug substances nominated but withdrawn', which FDA describes as bulk drug substances previously in category 2 of the interim policies that were withdrawn by the nominators. This page reads that as the nomination having been withdrawn by those who made it; FDA's page still gives a statement for each entry under its heading 'Potential significant safety risks'. FDA says on the same page that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nFDA's entry headed 'GHK-Cu (for injectable routes of administration)' says that compounded injectable drugs that contain GHK-Cu may pose risk for immunogenicity, and that there are limited data in humans to inform safety-related considerations. FDA's entry headed 'Epitalon' says that compounded drugs that contain epitalon may pose risk for immunogenicity for certain routes of administration, that it has not identified safety-related information regarding epitalon for the proposed route of administration, and that it therefore lacks sufficient information to know whether the drug would cause harm if administered to humans.\n\nFDA's entries are about the substances it names and drugs that contain them. None names a particular product or speaks of these substances combined, and nothing in them describes how this item is prepared or what it holds. FDA's GHK-Cu entry is about injectable routes of administration, and its other entry here names no particular route. The last section heading before this row in the catalog, on the page before it, is 'INJECTABLE & NASAL PEPTIDES'; the row itself states no route. The cautions carry every sentence of each entry.\n\nWhether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make. This listing does not state who prepares or supplies the item, how it is prepared, or whether it holds other ingredients; the dispensed product's own label governs. A prescription is required. Requests go through the provider pathway, where a licensed clinician decides whether it is appropriate. Fulfillment depends on state availability and pharmacy requirements, and it is not available for direct purchase.",
    "purpose": "The DailyMed search for this page returned no prescribing information under any name searched, so this page cites no labeled indication for the item. FDA's page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks has an entry under each listed name or a form of it, under its heading 'Potential significant safety risks'; the entries are not statements of any use of this item. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.",
    "cautions": "The statements below are FDA's, from its page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks, which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page. They are about the substances FDA names and drugs that contain them; FDA's entries name no particular product, and none speaks of these substances combined.\n\nOn GHK-Cu: FDA states that compounded injectable drugs containing GHK-Cu may pose risk for immunogenicity due to the potential for aggregation and peptide-related impurities. It states that there are limited data in humans to inform safety-related considerations.\n\nOn Epitalon: FDA states that compounded drugs containing epitalon may pose risk for immunogenicity for certain routes of administration due to the potential for aggregation and peptide-related impurities. It states that it has not identified safety-related information regarding epitalon for the proposed route of administration, and that it therefore lacks sufficient information to know whether the drug would cause harm if administered to humans.\n\nFDA places each of these entries in the list its page heads 'Bulk drug substances nominated but withdrawn', and describes that list as bulk drug substances previously in category 2 of the interim policies that were withdrawn by the nominators. This page reads that as the nomination having been withdrawn by those who made it; FDA's page still gives a statement for each entry under its heading 'Potential significant safety risks'. The same page says that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nThe listing's row names GHK-Cu and Epithalon; it does not state the item's sterility or what else it holds, and the dispensed product's own label and the prescribing clinician govern its use.",
    "contentReviewReceiptSha256": "159b5287692177670362077651a9828aee812c2bf71d3477908bdf21078e9da9",
