    "holdMessage": "Details for this listing have not yet been confirmed; a quote request is a question to our team and is not an order, a reservation or a prescription.",
    "summary": "Ibutamoren 12.5 mg capsule, as listed. FDA lists ibutamoren mesylate among bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks. A label search found no prescribing information, and a prescription is required. Clinical review required.",
    "overview": "In plain terms, the catalog lists this item as Ibutamoren 12.5 mg capsule; the row does not say what else it holds. A label search found no prescribing information, so this page states no use for the item and does not say how it works. FDA lists ibutamoren mesylate among bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks; what its entry says is set out below, and in full in the cautions. A licensed clinician reviews each request and decides whether it is appropriate.\n\nThe catalog lists this item as Ibutamoren 12.5 mg capsule. The listing's row gives that name, amount and form, without stating the route or what else the capsule holds.\n\nThe label search for this page, run on November 11, 2026 (UTC), looked up the listed name and returned two entries, each filed as a bulk ingredient listing for ibutamoren mesylate powder. Neither is prescribing information, and this page cites neither. FDA's drug application data, read the same day, returned no product with an active ingredient of that name. This page has no labeled indication to cite for the item; it cites no document but FDA's page and makes no statement about what Ibutamoren is used for or how it works.\n\nFDA's page 'Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks', which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page, has an entry headed 'Ibutamoren mesylate'. The listing's row says Ibutamoren alone, so it does not show whether the item is the mesylate FDA names.\n\nThe entry is in the list the page heads 'Bulk drug substances under category 2 of the interim policies', and the page's table marks it for both of the two policies it covers, with the dates it was added: one in December 2022 and one in September 2023. FDA says on the same page that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nFDA's entry says that ibutamoren mesylate poses significant safety risks due to the potential for congestive heart failure in certain patients. The cautions carry every sentence of the entry. FDA's entry is about the substance it names. It names no particular product, and nothing in it describes how this item is prepared or what it holds. The entry names no particular route of administration, and the listing's row states no route. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.\n\nThis listing does not state who prepares or supplies the capsule, how it is prepared, or whether it holds other ingredients; the dispensed product's own label governs. A prescription is required. Requests go through the provider pathway, where a licensed clinician decides whether it is appropriate. Fulfillment depends on state availability and pharmacy requirements, and it is not available for direct purchase.",
    "purpose": "The label search for this page returned no prescribing information under the listed name, so this page cites no labeled indication for the item. FDA's page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks has an entry for ibutamoren mesylate, under its heading 'Potential significant safety risks'; the entry is not a statement of any use of this item. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.",
    "cautions": "The statements below are FDA's, from its page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks, which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page. They are about the substance FDA names; FDA's entry names no particular product, and the listing's row states no route.\n\nFDA states that ibutamoren mesylate poses significant safety risks due to the potential for congestive heart failure in certain patients. It states that it is aware of a randomized, placebo-controlled trial assessing ibutamoren mesylate for the treatment of patients recovering from hip fracture that, in words FDA quotes, 'was terminated early due to a potential safety signal of congestive heart failure'.\n\nFDA places the entry in the list its page heads 'Bulk drug substances under category 2 of the interim policies', and marks it for both of the two policies the page's table covers, with the dates it was added: one in December 2022 and one in September 2023. The same page says that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nThe listing's row names Ibutamoren and a capsule; it does not state the route or what else the capsule holds, and the dispensed product's own label and the prescribing clinician govern its use.",
    "contentReviewReceiptSha256": "159b5287692177670362077651a9828aee812c2bf71d3477908bdf21078e9da9",
