    "summary": "Kisspeptin 1 mg/mL (5 mL), as listed. FDA lists Kisspeptin-10 among bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks. A label search found no prescribing information, and a prescription is required. Clinical review required.",
    "overview": "In plain terms, the catalog lists this item as Kisspeptin 1 mg/mL (5 mL); the row does not say what else it holds. A label search found no prescribing information, so this page states no use for the item and does not say how it works. FDA lists Kisspeptin-10 among bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks; what its entry says is set out below, and in full in the cautions. A licensed clinician reviews each request and decides whether it is appropriate.\n\nThe catalog lists this item as Kisspeptin 1 mg/mL (5 mL). The listing's row gives that name, concentration and volume, without stating the route or what else the item holds; the catalog counts the item in vials in its per-fill limit.\n\nA search of DailyMed drug labels for this page, run on November 11, 2026 (UTC), looked up the listed name and returned no label. FDA's drug application data, read the same day, list no product with an active ingredient of that name. This page has no labeled indication to cite for the item; it cites no document but FDA's page and makes no statement about what Kisspeptin is used for or how it works.\n\nFDA's page 'Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks', which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page, has an entry headed 'Kisspeptin-10'. The listing's row says Kisspeptin alone, so it does not show whether the item is the Kisspeptin-10 FDA names.\n\nThe entry is in the list the page heads 'Bulk drug substances under category 2 of the interim policies', and the page's table marks it for one of the two policies it covers and gives the date it was added as September 2023. FDA says on the same page that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nFDA's entry says that compounded drugs that contain Kisspeptin-10 may pose risk for immunogenicity for certain routes of administration, that it has no, or only limited, safety-related information for the proposed routes of administration, and that it therefore lacks sufficient information to know whether the drug would cause harm when administered to humans. The cautions carry every sentence of the entry.\n\nFDA's entry is about the substance it names and drugs that contain it. It names no particular product, and nothing in it describes how this item is prepared or what it holds. The entry speaks of routes of administration without naming one. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make. This listing does not state who prepares or supplies the item, how it is prepared, or whether it holds other ingredients; the dispensed product's own label governs.\n\nA prescription is required. Requests go through the provider pathway, where a licensed clinician decides whether it is appropriate. Fulfillment depends on state availability and pharmacy requirements, and it is not available for direct purchase.",
    "purpose": "The DailyMed search for this page returned no prescribing information under the listed name, so this page cites no labeled indication for the item. FDA's page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks has an entry for Kisspeptin-10, under its heading 'Potential significant safety risks'; the entry is not a statement of any use of this item. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.",
    "cautions": "The statements below are FDA's, from its page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks, which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page. They are about the substance FDA names and drugs that contain it; FDA's entry names no particular product.\n\nFDA states that compounded drugs containing Kisspeptin-10 may pose risk for immunogenicity for certain routes of administration and may have complexities with regard to peptide-related impurities and the characterization of the active pharmaceutical ingredient. It states that it has no, or only limited, safety-related information for the proposed routes of administration, and that it therefore lacks sufficient information to know whether the drug would cause harm when administered to humans.\n\nFDA places the entry in the list its page heads 'Bulk drug substances under category 2 of the interim policies', and marks it for one of the two policies the page's table covers and gives the date it was added as September 2023. The same page says that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nThe listing's row names Kisspeptin; it does not state the item's route or sterility or what else it holds, and the dispensed product's own label and the prescribing clinician govern its use.",
