    "summary": "Larazotide/KPV 500 mcg-500 mcg capsule, as listed. FDA lists KPV among bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks, and has no entry for larazotide. A label search found no prescribing information, and a prescription is required. Clinical review required.",
    "overview": "In plain terms, the catalog lists this item as Larazotide/KPV 500 mcg-500 mcg capsule; the row does not say in words which figure belongs to which name, or what else the item holds. A label search found no prescribing information, so this page states no use for the item and does not say how it works. FDA lists KPV among bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks, and has no entry for larazotide; what its entry says is set out below, and in full in the cautions. No document cited here is about larazotide. A licensed clinician reviews each request and decides whether it is appropriate.\n\nThe catalog lists this item as Larazotide/KPV and gives the figures 500 mcg-500 mcg. After the names the row gives figures joined by a hyphen; it does not say in words which figure belongs to which name, and this page prints them as listed. The row calls the item a capsule and does not state the route or what else the capsule holds.\n\nA search of DailyMed drug labels for this page, run on November 11, 2026 (UTC), looked up a name for each listed substance. It returned no label under Larazotide or KPV. FDA's drug application data, read the same day, list no product with an active ingredient of either name. This page has no labeled indication to cite for the item. No document cited here is about larazotide, and this page makes no statement of its own about what the item, or either substance named in it, is used for or how it works.\n\nFDA's page 'Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks', which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page, has an entry for one of the two listed names, headed 'KPV'; it has no entry for larazotide. The entry is in the list the page heads 'Bulk drug substances nominated but withdrawn', which FDA describes as bulk drug substances previously in category 2 of the interim policies that were withdrawn by the nominators.\n\nThis page reads that as the nomination having been withdrawn by those who made it; FDA's page still gives a statement for the entry under its heading 'Potential significant safety risks'. FDA says on the same page that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nFDA's entry headed 'KPV' says that it has not identified any human exposure data on drug products containing KPV, by any route of administration, and that it lacks important information regarding any safety issues raised by KPV, including whether it would cause harm if administered to humans.\n\nFDA's entry is about the substance it names and drugs that contain it. It names no particular product and does not speak of that substance combined with another, and nothing in it describes how this item is prepared or what it holds. FDA's KPV entry speaks of routes of administration without naming one, and the listing's row states no route. The cautions carry every sentence of the entry. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.\n\nThis listing does not state who prepares or supplies the capsule, how it is prepared, or whether it holds other ingredients; the dispensed product's own label governs. A prescription is required. Requests go through the provider pathway, where a licensed clinician decides whether it is appropriate. Fulfillment depends on state availability and pharmacy requirements, and it is not available for direct purchase.",
    "purpose": "The DailyMed search for this page returned no prescribing information under any name searched, so this page cites no labeled indication for the item. FDA's page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks has an entry for one of the two listed names, under its heading 'Potential significant safety risks'; the entry is not a statement of any use of this item. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.",
    "cautions": "The statements below are FDA's, from its page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks, which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page. FDA's statements are about the substance FDA names and drugs that contain it; its entry names no particular product and does not speak of that substance combined with another. No document cited here is about larazotide.\n\nOn KPV: FDA states that it has not identified any human exposure data on drug products containing KPV administered via any route of administration. It states that it lacks important information regarding any safety issues raised by KPV, including whether it would cause harm if administered to humans.\n\nFDA places the entry in the list its page heads 'Bulk drug substances nominated but withdrawn', and describes that list as bulk drug substances previously in category 2 of the interim policies that were withdrawn by the nominators. This page reads that as the nomination having been withdrawn by those who made it; FDA's page still gives a statement for the entry under its heading 'Potential significant safety risks'. The same page says that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nThe listing's row names Larazotide and KPV and a capsule; it does not state what else the capsule holds, and the dispensed product's own label and the prescribing clinician govern its use.",
    "contentReviewReceiptSha256": "159b5287692177670362077651a9828aee812c2bf71d3477908bdf21078e9da9",
