    "holdCode": "risk_review",
    "holdMessage": "Details for this listing have not yet been confirmed; a quote request is a question to our team and is not an order, a reservation or a prescription.",
    "summary": "Sermorelin/Ipamorelin 3 mg-3.6 mg/mL (5 mL), as listed. FDA lists ipamorelin acetate among bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks, and has no entry for sermorelin. A label search found no prescribing information, and a prescription is required. Clinical review required.",
    "overview": "In plain terms, the catalog lists this item as Sermorelin/Ipamorelin 3 mg-3.6 mg/mL (5 mL); the row does not say in words which figure belongs to which name, or what else the item holds. A label search found no prescribing information, so this page states no use for the item and does not say how it works. FDA lists ipamorelin acetate among bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks, and has no entry for sermorelin; what its entry says is set out below, and in full in the cautions. No document cited here says what sermorelin is or does. A licensed clinician reviews each request and decides whether it is appropriate.\n\nThe catalog lists this item as Sermorelin/Ipamorelin and gives the figures 3 mg-3.6 mg/mL (5 mL). After the names the row gives figures joined by a hyphen; it does not say in words which figure belongs to which name, and this page prints them as listed. The row does not state the route or what else the item holds; the catalog counts the item in vials in its per-fill limit.\n\nA search of DailyMed drug labels for this page, run on November 11, 2026 (UTC), looked up a name for each listed substance. It returned no label under Sermorelin or Ipamorelin. FDA's drug application data, read the same day, list no product with an active ingredient named Ipamorelin, and list three sermorelin acetate products under two applications, each marked discontinued; on each, the data carry FDA's note of a Federal Register determination that the product was not discontinued or withdrawn for safety or effectiveness reasons.\n\nThose are other finished products, which the listing does not say this item is, and the note is about them. The listing's row says Sermorelin alone, so it does not show whether the item holds the acetate. This page has no labeling to cite for the item or for either listed substance, and cites no labeled indication for the item. No document cited here says what sermorelin is or does, and this page makes no statement of its own about what the item, or either substance named in it, is used for or how it works.\n\nFDA's page 'Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks', which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page, has an entry under one of the two listed names, in the form 'Ipamorelin acetate'; it has no entry for sermorelin. The listing's row says Ipamorelin alone, so it does not show whether the item holds the acetate FDA names.\n\nIpamorelin acetate has an entry in the list the page heads 'Bulk drug substances under category 2 of the interim policies' and another in the list it heads 'Bulk drug substances nominated but withdrawn', which FDA describes as bulk drug substances previously in category 2 of the interim policies that were withdrawn by the nominators; FDA's heading for its entry in the withdrawn list says it also appears in the other table because it is in category 2 under one of the interim policies.\n\nFDA says on the same page that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nFDA's entry headed 'Ipamorelin acetate' says that compounded drugs that contain ipamorelin acetate may pose risk for immunogenicity for certain routes of administration, that a study published in literature identified serious adverse events including death when ipamorelin was administered intravenously for improving gastric motility, and that it has not identified safety-related information regarding ipamorelin acetate via certain other injectable routes of administration.\n\nFDA's entry is about the substance it names and drugs that contain it. It names no particular product and does not speak of that substance combined with another, and nothing in it describes how this item is prepared or what it holds. FDA's ipamorelin acetate entry speaks of intravenous and certain other injectable routes of administration. The last section heading before this row in the catalog, on the page before it, is 'INJECTABLE & NASAL PEPTIDES'; the row itself states no route. The cautions carry every sentence of the entry.\n\nWhether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make. This listing does not state who prepares or supplies the item, how it is prepared, or whether it holds other ingredients; the dispensed product's own label governs. A prescription is required. Requests go through the provider pathway, where a licensed clinician decides whether it is appropriate. Fulfillment depends on state availability and pharmacy requirements, and it is not available for direct purchase.",
    "purpose": "The DailyMed search for this page returned no prescribing information under any name searched, so this page cites no labeled indication for the item. FDA's page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks has an entry under one of the two listed names, in the form 'Ipamorelin acetate', under its heading 'Potential significant safety risks'; the entry is not a statement of any use of this item. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.",
    "cautions": "The statements below are FDA's, from its page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks, which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page. FDA's statements are about the substance FDA names and drugs that contain it; its entry names no particular product and does not speak of that substance combined with another. FDA's page has no entry for sermorelin, and no labeling was found for it.\n\nOn Ipamorelin acetate: FDA states that compounded drugs containing ipamorelin acetate may pose risk for immunogenicity for certain routes of administration due to the potential for aggregation or peptide-related impurities, and that ipamorelin acetate also contains unnatural amino acids, which add to the complexity of peptide characterization. It states that a study published in literature identified serious adverse events including death when ipamorelin was administered intravenously for improving gastric motility. It states that it has not identified safety-related information regarding ipamorelin acetate via certain other injectable routes of administration, and that it therefore lacks sufficient information to know whether the drug would cause harm if administered to humans via those routes.\n\nOn FDA's page ipamorelin acetate has an entry in the list the page heads 'Bulk drug substances under category 2 of the interim policies' and another in the list it heads 'Bulk drug substances nominated but withdrawn', which FDA describes as bulk drug substances previously in category 2 of the interim policies that were withdrawn by the nominators; FDA's heading for its entry in the withdrawn list says it also appears in the other table because it is in category 2 under one of the interim policies. The same page says that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nThe listing's row names Sermorelin and Ipamorelin; it does not state the item's sterility or what else it holds, and the dispensed product's own label and the prescribing clinician govern its use.",
    "contentReviewReceiptSha256": "159b5287692177670362077651a9828aee812c2bf71d3477908bdf21078e9da9",
