    "summary": "Thymosin Alpha-1 3 mg/mL, 5 mL, as listed. FDA lists thymosin alpha-1 among bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks. A label search found no prescribing information, and a prescription is required. Clinical review required.",
    "overview": "In plain terms, the catalog lists this item as Thymosin Alpha-1 3 mg/mL, 5 mL; the row does not say what else it holds. A label search found no prescribing information, so this page states no use for the item and does not say how it works. FDA lists thymosin alpha-1 among bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks; what its entry says is set out below, and in full in the cautions. A licensed clinician reviews each request and decides whether it is appropriate.\n\nThe catalog lists this item as Thymosin Alpha-1 at 3 mg/mL, in a 5 mL size. The listing's row gives that name, concentration and volume, without stating the route or what else the item holds; the catalog counts the item in vials in its per-fill limit.\n\nThe label search for this page, run on November 11, 2026 (UTC), looked up the name thymosin alpha and one other name and returned no label. FDA's drug application data, read the same day, returned no product with an active ingredient of the name thymosin or that other name. This page has no labeled indication to cite for the item; it cites no document but FDA's page and makes no statement about what Thymosin Alpha-1 is used for or how it works.\n\nFDA's page 'Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks', which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page, has an entry headed 'Thymosin-alpha 1 (Ta1)'. The listing's row says Thymosin Alpha-1. The entry is in the list the page heads 'Bulk drug substances nominated but withdrawn', which FDA describes as bulk drug substances previously in category 2 of the interim policies that were withdrawn by the nominators.\n\nThis page reads that as the nomination having been withdrawn by those who made it; FDA's page still gives a statement of potential significant safety risks for the entry. FDA says on the same page that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nFDA's entry says that compounded drugs that contain thymosin alpha-1 may pose significant risk for immunogenicity for certain routes of administration, and that the safety-related information is inadequate for the agency to sufficiently understand the extent of any safety issues raised by the proposed drug. The cautions carry every sentence of the entry.\n\nFDA's entry is about the substance it names and drugs that contain it. It names no particular product, and nothing in it describes how this item is prepared or what it holds. The entry names no particular route of administration, and the listing's row states no route. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.\n\nThis listing does not state who prepares or supplies the item, how it is prepared, or whether it holds other ingredients; the dispensed product's own label governs. A prescription is required. Requests go through the provider pathway, where a licensed clinician decides whether it is appropriate. Fulfillment depends on state availability and pharmacy requirements, and it is not available for direct purchase.",
    "purpose": "The label search for this page returned no prescribing information under the name thymosin alpha and one other name, so this page cites no labeled indication for the item. FDA's page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks has an entry for thymosin alpha-1, under its heading 'Potential significant safety risks'; the entry is not a statement of any use of this item. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.",
    "cautions": "The statements below are FDA's, from its page on bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks, which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page. They are about the substance FDA names and drugs that contain it; FDA's entry names no particular product, and the listing's row states no route.\n\nFDA states that compounded drugs containing thymosin alpha-1 may pose significant risk for immunogenicity for certain routes of administration and may have complexities with regard to peptide-related impurities and the characterization of the active pharmaceutical ingredient. It states that the safety-related information is inadequate for the agency to sufficiently understand the extent of any safety issues raised by the proposed compounded drug.\n\nFDA places the entry in the list its page heads 'Bulk drug substances nominated but withdrawn', and describes that list as bulk drug substances previously in category 2 of the interim policies that were withdrawn by the nominators. This page reads that as the nomination having been withdrawn by those who made it; FDA's page still gives a statement of potential significant safety risks for the entry. The same page says that bulk drug substances that may present significant safety risks have been placed in category 2 under the interim policies.\n\nThe listing's row names Thymosin Alpha-1; it does not state the item's route or sterility or what else it holds, and the dispensed product's own label and the prescribing clinician govern its use.",
