    "holdMessage": "Details for this listing have not yet been confirmed; a quote request is a question to our team and is not an order, a reservation or a prescription.",
    "summary": "Oxandrolone 50 mg capsule, as listed; a prescription is required. Oxandrin's labeling, cited here as that of a different product, says anabolic steroids are synthetic derivatives of testosterone and that oxandrolone is a Schedule III controlled substance. In 2023 FDA determined that Oxandrin (oxandrolone) tablets were withdrawn from sale for reasons of safety or effectiveness. Clinical review required.",
    "overview": "In plain terms, the catalog lists this item as Oxandrolone 50 mg capsule; the row does not say what else the capsule holds. The one label a search returned is for Oxandrin, an oxandrolone tablet, which is a different finished product; its labeling says anabolic steroids are synthetic derivatives of testosterone and that oxandrolone is a Schedule III controlled substance. In 2023 FDA withdrew approval of the application for Oxandrin tablets and of four other applications for oxandrolone tablets, and said it had determined that Oxandrin tablets were withdrawn from sale for reasons of safety or effectiveness; what its notices say is set out below, and in the cautions. That labeling carries a boxed warning about the liver and blood lipids and contraindications that include pregnancy; the cautions give the details. Neither the labeling nor the notices name this item or describe a capsule, and this page cites no labeled use for the item. A licensed clinician reviews each request and decides whether it is appropriate.\n\nThe catalog lists this item as Oxandrolone 50 mg capsule. The listing's row gives that name, an amount and the word capsule, without stating the route or what else the capsule holds.\n\nThe label search for this page, run on November 10, 2026 (UTC), looked up the name oxandrolone and returned one label: prescribing information for Oxandrin, an oxandrolone tablet, in a version effective April 9, 2007. This page cites it as the labeling of a different finished product: Oxandrin is a tablet and the listing is a capsule. FDA's drug application data, read November 11, 2026, list eight oxandrolone tablet products under five abbreviated new drug applications, give every one the marketing status discontinued, and list no product named Oxandrin.\n\nThree Federal Register notices of the Food and Drug Administration are cited for what happened to Oxandrin tablets and to other oxandrolone tablets. In a notice published June 28, 2023, FDA withdrew approval of the application for Oxandrin tablets and of four other applications for oxandrolone tablets, as of that date. The notice says the holders voluntarily requested the withdrawal, and that FDA had told them it believed a potential problem associated with oxandrolone tablets was sufficiently serious that the drug products should be removed from the market.\n\nIn a notice published September 13, 2023, FDA said it had determined that Oxandrin tablets were withdrawn from sale for reasons of safety or effectiveness. The notice says an FDA advisory committee that met in January 1984 unanimously concluded that there was no evidence of efficacy for oxandrolone, that the agency concluded the benefit-risk profile of the drug product is unfavorable without substantial evidence to support effectiveness, and that the drug products would not be considered safe and effective if they were reintroduced to the market today. A correction published October 7, 2026 says FDA made the same determination for the other oxandrolone tablet applications that used Oxandrin as their reference.\n\nThe Oxandrin prescribing information says anabolic steroids are synthetic derivatives of testosterone, that complete dissociation of anabolic and androgenic effects has not been achieved, and that oxandrolone is classified as a controlled substance under the Anabolic Steroids Control Act of 1990 and has been assigned to Schedule III (non-narcotic).\n\nThe labeled product is a tablet, in two strengths, neither of which is the listed one. Its labeling carries a boxed warning about the liver and blood lipids, contraindications that include pregnancy, and the statement that anabolic steroids have not been shown to enhance athletic ability. The cautions give the details.\n\nNeither that labeling nor the notices name this item or describe a capsule, and none is a statement about how this item is prepared. The labeled use of Oxandrin tablets was a use of that product and is not a labeled use of this item; nothing in the listing's row shows whether statements about oxandrolone tablets apply to this item. The notices' statements on efficacy and on safety concerns are about oxandrolone, the name the listing gives, and the boxed warning is worded about androgenic anabolic steroid therapy.\n\nWhether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make. This listing does not state who prepares or supplies the capsule, how it is prepared, or whether it holds other ingredients; the dispensed product's own label governs. A prescription is required. Requests go through the provider pathway, where a licensed clinician decides whether it is appropriate. Fulfillment depends on state availability and pharmacy requirements, and it is not available for direct purchase.",
