    "holdCode": "offering_decision",
    "holdMessage": "Details for this listing have not yet been confirmed; a quote request is a question to our team and is not an order, a reservation or a prescription.",
    "summary": "Stanozolol 5 mg capsule, as listed; a prescription is required. Federal regulations list stanozolol in Schedule III as an anabolic steroid, a class they define by its chemical and pharmacological relation to testosterone. No label was found; FDA's application data list one stanozolol product, a tablet, marked discontinued. Clinical review required.",
    "overview": "In plain terms, the catalog lists this item as Stanozolol 5 mg capsule; the row does not say what else the capsule holds. Federal regulations list stanozolol in Schedule III, under the heading 'Anabolic steroids', and define an anabolic steroid by its chemical and pharmacological relation to testosterone; the full definition is given below. A label search found no label for it, and FDA's application data list one stanozolol product, a tablet, marked discontinued, which is a different finished product. No document cited here says what stanozolol is used for or how it works, or states a warning, a contraindication or an adverse effect for this item; that is an absence of documents, not a finding that the item is without risk. A licensed clinician reviews each request and decides whether it is appropriate.\n\nThe catalog lists this item as Stanozolol 5 mg capsule. The listing's row gives that name, an amount and the word capsule, without stating the route or what else the capsule holds. A search of DailyMed drug labels for this page, run on November 11, 2026 (UTC), looked up the listed name and one other name and returned no label.\n\nFDA's drug application data, read the same day, list one stanozolol product, a tablet, under one application, marked discontinued; the data read for this page give no reason for it. It is another finished product, which the listing does not say this item is; the listing is a capsule. FDA's page 'Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks', which gives its content as current as of April 22, 2026 in the copy read for this page, has no entry under that name.\n\nTitle 21 of the Code of Federal Regulations, read on November 11, 2026 in the government's electronic edition, lists stanozolol in Schedule III, under the heading 'Anabolic steroids' (section 1308.13); the section says each drug or substance in it has been assigned a DEA Controlled Substances Code Number. Section 1300.01 of the same title defines an anabolic steroid as any drug or hormonal substance, chemically and pharmacologically related to testosterone, other than estrogens, progestins, corticosteroids and dehydroepiandrosterone. The regulation names the substance; it does not name this item or say what the substance is used for.\n\nSo this page has no labeling to cite about what Stanozolol is used for or how it works, and it makes no statement of its own about either. It draws no conclusion from what the searches returned or did not return: neither is a finding about the item. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.\n\nThis listing does not state who prepares or supplies the capsule, how it is prepared, or whether it holds other ingredients; the dispensed product's own label governs. A prescription is required. Requests go through the provider pathway, where a licensed clinician decides whether it is appropriate. Fulfillment depends on state availability and pharmacy requirements, and it is not available for direct purchase.",
    "purpose": "The DailyMed search for this page returned no prescribing information for a human product under the name searched, so this page cites no labeled indication for the item, and no document cited here states a use for it. Whether this item is appropriate for a given patient is a clinical decision that this listing does not make.",
    "cautions": "No document cited on this page states a warning, a contraindication or an adverse effect for this item: no labeling was found for it, FDA's page has no entry under the name, and the regulation cited lists the substance in a schedule without describing its effects. That is an absence of documents and is not a finding that the item is without risk.\n\nThe listing's row names Stanozolol and a capsule; it does not state what else the capsule holds, and the dispensed product's own label and the prescribing clinician govern its use.",
